{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "3 - Hydro Consumers",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Introduction",
      "description": "## Hydro Consumers\n\nMost of the work involved in delivering notifications is performed by a series of hydro consumers. The hydro docs for consumers are [here](https://hydro.githubapp.com/docs/consumers).\n\nIn short, a hydro consumer receives a message from a particular hydro \"topic\" one message at a time, processes it, then moves on to the next message. Each topic contains different types of messages, and each message type will match a defined [hydro schema](https://github.com/github/hydro-schemas).\n\nFor this code tour we will look at how processing the `notifyd.v0.DeliverMobilePush` messages works. The schema for these messages are defined here: https://github.com/github/hydro-schemas/blob/master/proto/hydro/schemas/notifyd/v0/deliver_mobile_push.proto"
    },
    {
      "file": "hydro/schemas/notifyd/v0/deliver_mobile_push.pb.go",
      "description": "## Importing hydro schemas\n\nWhen schemas in the `github/hydro-schemas` are created or updated, we import them into this repo using [the vendoring instructions](https://hydro.githubapp.com/docs/adding_an_event#workflow_vendor_schema).\n\nThis vendoring process creates autogenerated code like this file in our repository. This code is go code that can be used to marshal and unmarshal messages of this schema in our application.\n\nYou should have no need to edit these files, but they can be useful as a reference and your editor should be able to find them for autocompletion.",
      "line": 25
    },
    {
      "title": "Anatomy of a consumer",
      "description": "## Anatomy of a consumer\n\nA \"consumer\" exists at multiple levels of abstraction.\n\n- an `internal/` go package\n    - this is where the business logic should go for handling the relevant hydro message and doing something with it\n- a `cmd/` binary\n    - this is the binary that will actually run\n    - it is responsible for configuring the internal consumer, and hooking it up to the `hydro-client`'s kafka consumer\n- a kubernetes deployment\n    - this will setup an actual deployment in kubernetes to run the `cmd`\n\nWe will look at these in this order."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/mobile/delivery/consumer.go",
      "description": "## HandleMessage\n\nThis is the most interesting function in the consumer. It is where the hydro message (already unmarshalled into the correct message schema) is received and processed.\n\nIt's only return value is an error. All business logic goes here. In this case we lookup the recipient's mobile device tokens, render the notification content, and deliver the notification to each device.",
      "pattern": "func .* HandleMessage\\("
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/mobile/delivery/consumer.go",
      "description": "## Error handling\n\nError handling is extremely important.\n\nYou have two options:\n\n1. log and return `nil`\n    - this will discard the current message and move on\n2. return an error\n    - this will return the message to be retried\n\nYou should only return an error if you actually want to retry processing this message, i.e. if:\n\n- if something is very wrong at a system level and you think we should retry\n- something is temporarily wrong that may actually recover\n\nDue to hydro/kafka's head of line blocking, we can't move on to the next message until we've processed or discarded the other one, so returning an error for something irrecoverable could cause us to get blocked forever.",
      "pattern": "return err"
    }
  ]
}
